===Noun===
{{examples|
* Dutch: {{m|nl|beamer|g=m||video projector}}
* German: {{m|de|Handy|g=n||mobile phone}}
* French: {{m|fr|auto-stop|g=m||hitchhiking}}, {{m|fr|baby-foot|g=m||table football}}, {{m|fr|footing|g=m||jogging}}
* Italian: {{m|it|smart working|g=m||t=work from home, remote working}}, {{m|it|baby parking|g=m||t=childcare}}
* Spanish: {{m|es|playback|g=m||lip-syncing}}
}}
{{en-noun}}

# A word in a language other than English that has an English appearance but is not current in English itself. This may be, for example, because the word was borrowed from a [[neologism]] that never entered English common use, or because it was independently derived from an English word, or because it was deliberately [[coin]]ed to resemble English.

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|pseudo-English}}
* {{l|en|pseudo-Latinism}}, {{l|en|pseudo-latinism}}

